      "supported_claims": [
        "The benchmark assesses performance on unfamiliar grid transformations: worked examples precede new inputs requiring complete output grids.",
        "Scoring is explicit: two attempts, exact matching per input, within-problem averaging and then averaging across problems.",
        "Development included human solving and duplicate-rule review: 407 people completed 515 sessions, although the pool exceeded the final test.",
        "The three test sets were matched for human difficulty; this supports human difficulty comparability only.",
        "Verified evaluations record settings and impose spending controls; public materials and dated corrections support inspection and version tracking."
      ],
      "documented_problems": [
        "Some coloured squares and unclear examples required corrections, as recorded in the public change history.",
        "Partial problem scores can differ from the published description of a problem as fully solved, requiring careful terminology.",
        "Verified testing uses one run, leaving its reported score without supplied repeat-run uncertainty.",
        "Public evaluation problems are exposed, and hidden-data policies acknowledge that information could escape.",
        "The change record does not recalculate every affected AI score, limiting comparisons across revisions."
      ],
      "missing_information": [
        "AI repeat-run distributions, standard errors and uncertainty for score differences are not supplied.",
        "AI alternate-form equivalence is not established by human difficulty matching.",
        "Final-pool item statistics, detailed selection criteria and expert qualifications or agreement are not supplied.",
        "Systematic effects of instructions, grid encoding and software changes are not supplied.",
        "External validity for language, social interaction, real-world work or comprehensive intelligence is not supplied.",
